Date: Sat, 12 Sep 2026 15:28:36 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 212/221] feat(area): water volume function component (area +
 river)

"Water Volume" in Add Component -> Area. It binds by rule to the one shape
component on the entity and picks its mode from the shape's kind: a Polygon
becomes area water, a Spline becomes a river, anything else is a latched warning
and no water.

Area water is decision 05 option C - an entity SLOT render node. The component
creates an IWaterVolumeRenderNode, calls SetAreaAttachedToEntity() and hands it
to IEntity::SetSlotRenderNode, so hide, layer hiding, selection highlight and the
world matrix come from the slot and MOVING THE ENTITY IS A MATRIX UPDATE. The
legacy CWaterShapeObject re-tessellates on every transform invalidation
(ShapeObject.cpp:1044-1053) and CreateArea tessellates 256x
(WaterVolumeRenderNode.cpp:262-322). The vertices are local, relative to contour
point 0, and the slot's local transform carries that point - which reproduces the
legacy fog plane (world Z column through world point 0) exactly.

Physics is hand-rolled, because Physicalize() early-returns for an attached node
(.cpp:840): SetAndCreatePhysicsArea, then pe_params_pos / pe_params_buoyancy /
pe_params_foreign_data, the recipe of CGameVolume_Water::CreatePhysicsArea
(GameVolume_Water.cpp:418-470) - plus the two things that game object never had,
SetPhysParams' density and resistance and the whole pe_params_area wave-sim
block. Moving the entity moves the area with pe_params_pos alone.

A river is N nodes, one per sector, as CRiverObject builds them
(RiverObject.cpp:195-271), with one stitched physics contour on sector 0. They
are NOT slot-attached, because a river node's physics area can only be built by
Physicalize() - SetAndCreatePhysicsArea goes through SetAreaPhysicsArea, which
refuses anything that is not eWVT_Area - and Physicalize() refuses anything that
IS attached. They are still owned by the entity, which is what keeps the octree
exporter from baking them.

Full property parity with CWaterShapeObject, including the advanced wave-sim
block, and the eST_Water shader check both legacy objects make. The legacy
CausticTiling load bug (WaterShapeObject.cpp:396) is not reproduced. Rebuilds are
guarded by a parameter digest, so an open inspector panel does not re-tessellate
the volume many times a second.

